{"reference":"Mark 12","book":"Mark","chapter":12,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Questions About Tribute","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The question about giving tribute to Caesar concerns imperial tribute. Gospel passages use different settings and vocabulary for imperial tribute, toll collection, and temple-associated payments, so these should not automatically be treated as one undifferentiated kind of tax.","verse_refs":["Mark 12:13-17"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_mark_012_ca469aaaedf21f1af90c4d0e"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Economic and Religious Debates","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The chapter’s tenants, tribute, wealthy donors, and poor widow belong to a world marked by unequal agrarian and imperial economies. Its debate over the greatest commandment is an argument within a Jewish scriptural world, not evidence that Judaism was uniquely legalistic. The widow’s gift may be read as exemplary, as exposing a system that consumes widows’ houses, or as doing both; the passage cannot require vulnerable people to surrender necessities to institutions.","verse_refs":["Mark 12:1-44","Mark 12:28-34","Mark 12:38-44"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_mark_012_b842da9c66cb33a0173de14e"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}